      "id": 1,
      "text": "Akron was founded in 1825 by Simon Perkins and Paul Williams atop the Ohio and Erie Canal summit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akron%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Akron earned the nickname “Rubber Capital of the World” thanks to giants like Goodyear, Goodrich, Firestone, and General Tire.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akron%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Seiberling brothers founded Goodyear Tire & Rubber in 1898, naming it after Charles Goodyear, the vulcanization inventor.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.summithistory.org/historic-akron-sites",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Stan Hywet Hall and Gardens, a 70‑acre National Historic Landmark, once housed Frank Seiberling and now serves as a museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akron%2C_Ohio",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Akron preserves John Brown’s legacy with the John Brown House and Monument, commemorating the abolitionist’s residence here.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
